# The class above has a setter for the name. An object that the constructor function made in front of `Object.setPrototypeOf(Sub.prototype, Base.prototype)` finds no setter: the store lays a property of the object's own.
#   class Base { set level(v) { sink(v); } }  function Sub() { this.a = 1; }  const early = new Sub();  early.level = 5;
#   Object.setPrototypeOf(Sub.prototype, Base.prototype);  function read(o) { return o.level; }  sink(read(early));
# When the store ended at the setter, what is stored reached no node and the layout got no such name: the run time stopped at "AddProperty of an object of layout L1: the compiler has proven that it never happens".
# Two places decide it: the flow, which leads what is stored to whoever reads the name, and the writer of the layouts' tables, which says that such an object may gain a property.
